# PMDown

PMDown is a lightweight Markdown parser that converts a Markdown file into an HTML file.

## Requirements

JDK 27

## Usage

Build the application with:

```powershell
.\build.ps1
```

Convert a file with:

```text
java -jar PMDown.jar <input-file> [output-file]
```

If you omit the output file, PMDown creates an HTML file with the same base name in the input file's directory. If the output path does not already have an `.html` extension (case-insensitive), PMDown appends it.

To display the help message, run `java -jar PMDown.jar --help` or `java -jar PMDown.jar -h`.

## Supported Markdown Syntax

1.Use `\\` to escape the next character. Any character can be escaped.
Use one to six consecutive `#` characters to set a heading level from H1 to H6.
Use `[Display Text](URL)` to create a link. Relative links are allowed. Absolute links support the `http:`, `https:`, and `mailto:` schemes; unsupported schemes and invalid URLs are rendered as plain text.
Use `*Text*` for italic text.
Use `**Text**` for bold text.
Use `~~Text~~` for strikethrough text.
Use `$formula$` for an inline math formula. Formulas are rendered with MathJax using the `\(formula\)` delimiter format.
Use `\`code\`` for inline code.
Use triple backticks to create a fenced code block. An optional language label on the opening fence is displayed above the code; syntax highlighting is not applied.
Use tables and lists as shown in the examples below. Inline Markdown formatting is supported in table cells.
Separate paragraphs with a blank line.

PMDown uses a custom Markdown grammar. Use the `.pmdown` extension for files written in this grammar to distinguish them from standard Markdown files.

## Examples

```pmdown
# Example

This is an example.

This is the first line.  
This line will appear on the same line as the previous one.

This line will appear in a new paragraph.

Table examples:

| Title 1 | Title 2 |
| Content for Title 1 |
| --- | --- |
| The separator must contain just right three hyphens (`---`). Missing cells are rendered as empty; extra cells are ignored. | This cell is valid | Extra cells like this one are ignored |
| The cell to the right of this one is empty |
| --- |
| Content after the second separator row is ignored. |

Table without separator row:
| Title   | Title   |
| Content | Content |
| All rows in this table are rendered as plain text because no separator row is present. | All rows in this table are rendered as plain text because no separator row is present. |

An ordered list:
1.You can start an ordered list with the `1.` prefix.
You do not need to write `2.`, `3.`, and so on on the following lines.
Each line is treated as a separate list item.
To end the list, start a new paragraph.

Like this.

An unordered list:
-.You can start an unordered list with the `-.` prefix.
The same continuation rules apply as for ordered lists.

Here is some `inline code`, followed by a Java code block:
\`\`\`java
if ((args.length > 0 && (args[0].equals("-h") || args[0].equals("--help"))) || args.length == 0) {
    showHelp();
    return;
}
\`\`\`
```

